02 — Ubuntu Server VM Setup
What is this VM for?
This Ubuntu Server VM is the main workhorse of the homelab. It runs Docker, which hosts all the self-hosted services (Pi-hole, Portainer, etc.) as containers.

Downloading the ISO

In the Proxmox web UI, navigate to local (pve) → ISO Images
Click Download from URL and paste:

   https://releases.ubuntu.com/24.04/ubuntu-24.04.2-live-server-amd64.iso

Click Query URL then Download — Proxmox downloads it directly, nothing touches your local machine


Creating the VM

Click Create VM in the top right of the Proxmox UI
Use these settings:

Name ubuntu-server 
OS Select the Ubuntu ISO downloaded above 
SystemLeave default
(SeaBIOS) Disk32GB+ on local-lvm 
CPU 2 cores
Memory2048 MB (2GB) 
Network vmbr0 (default bridge)

Click Finish — don't start it yet, review the summary first


Installing Ubuntu Server

Start the VM and click Console in Proxmox to open the display
Work through the installer:


Installation type: Ubuntu Server (not minimized)
Network: Leave as default DHCP
Storage: Use the entire disk, keep LVM enabled
Profile: Set your username and password
OpenSSH: Enable Install OpenSSH Server — critical for remote access
Featured Snaps: Skip all, install nothing here


Wait for the install to complete, then reboot when prompted


First Boot
Once the VM reboots, log in via the Proxmox console and get the VM's IP:
baship a
Look for the 10.0.0.x address on the ens18 (or similar) interface.

SSH In From Mac
From this point on, manage the VM entirely from Terminal on your Mac — no need to use the Proxmox console.
bashssh yourusername@10.0.0.x
Accept the fingerprint prompt the first time. You're in!

Update the System
First thing after logging in:
bashsudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y

Verify it Appears on the Network
From your computer, confirm the VM shows up on the local network:
arp -a
It should appear as a separate device from the Proxmox host.
